# ctxloom private working state (synced bundles, session/context, project id)
# Codex runtime state. ctxloom points CODEX_HOME at <project>/.codex to keep
# prompts cell-local (internal/codex/surfaces.go), but codex uses ONE home for
# both its config surface AND all of its runtime state — so it also writes its
# session store, conversation memories, logs, goals, temp files, AND ITS OWN
# bundled system skills (.codex/skills/.system/*) here. config.toml is now
# EXCLUDED from the tracked exceptions below (white-dawn §2.2B): codex itself
# appends a machine-specific `[projects."<abs-path>"] trust_level` entry into
# it on first run, so a file codex mutates every run should not be tracked —
# matches internal/gitignore/gitignore.go's TransientArtifactPatterns, which
# already ignores .codex/config.toml for every OTHER project's generated
# .gitignore; this repo's own hand-authored block had drifted from that
# policy (it used to un-ignore config.toml) and a `[projects...]` entry for
# this very repo had already leaked into git history before this fix — see
# the task's final report. skills/ stays fully ignored too (unlike prompts/,
# it is NOT ctxloom-exclusive — codex ships its own .system/ skills there, so
# there is no clean "ctxloom-owned" subset to un-ignore). Only the
# ctxloom-generated prompts (never codex-mutated) stay tracked; everything
# else is engine state that must never be committed.
.codex/*
!.codex/prompts/

# ctxloom private working state (synced bundles, session/context, project id)
.agents/

